React is an open-source JavaScript library developed by Facebook for building user interfaces, particularly for single-page applications. It allows developers to create reusable UI components that manage their own state, making it easier to build interactive and dynamic web applications. React uses a component-based architecture, enabling developers to break down complex UIs into smaller, manageable pieces. It is highly performant due to its use of the Virtual DOM, which minimizes direct manipulation of the actual DOM. React is widely used for its simplicity, flexibility, and strong community support.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';
const App = () => <h1>Hello, React!</h1>;
ReactDOM.render(<App />, document.getElementById('root'));
    </pre>
    This code renders a simple "Hello, React!" heading to the DOM.

Components are the building blocks of a React application, representing reusable pieces of the UI. They can be either <strong>class-based</strong> (using ES6 classes) or <strong>functional</strong> (using functions). Components accept inputs called <strong>props</strong> and manage their own <strong>state</strong> to render dynamic UI. Functional components are preferred in modern React, especially with Hooks for state and side effects. Components promote modularity and reusability, making code easier to maintain.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React from 'react';
const Greeting = ({ name }) => <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;
export default Greeting;
    </pre>
    This functional component accepts a 'name' prop and renders a greeting.

State is an object that holds dynamic data in a React component, allowing it to update and re-render when the data changes. State is managed within a component (or via state management libraries like Redux). In functional components, the <strong>useState</strong> Hook is used to manage state. State changes trigger re-renders, making the UI reactive.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { useState } from 'react';
const Counter = () => {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <div>
      <p>Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
    </div>
  );
};
    </pre>
    This component tracks and updates a counter using state.

The <strong>useEffect</strong> Hook allows functional components to perform side effects, such as fetching data, updating the DOM, or setting up subscriptions. It runs after every render by default but can be controlled with a dependency array to run only when specific values change.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
const DataFetcher = () => {
  const [data, setData] = useState([]);
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch('https://api.example.com/data')
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(setData);
  }, []); // Empty array means run once on mount
  return <div>{data.map(item => <p>{item.name}</p>)}</div>;
};
    </pre>
    This fetches data on component mount.

React Router is a library for handling navigation and routing in React applications. It enables dynamic, client-side routing, allowing users to navigate between different views without full page reloads. Key components include <strong>BrowserRouter</strong>, <strong>Route</strong>, and <strong>Link</strong>.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import { BrowserRouter, Route, Link } from 'react-router-dom';
const App = () => (
  <BrowserRouter>
    <nav><Link to="/home">Home</Link></nav>
    <Route path="/home" component={() => <h1>Home Page</h1>} />
  </BrowserRouter>
);
    </pre>
    This sets up basic routing with a link to a home page.

React Hooks are functions that let functional components use state, lifecycle methods, and other React features without classes. Introduced in React 16.8, Hooks like <strong>useState</strong>, <strong>useEffect</strong>, and <strong>useContext</strong> simplify code and improve reusability.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { useState } from 'react';
const Toggle = () => {
  const [isOn, setIsOn] = useState(false);
  return <button onClick={() => setIsOn(!isOn)}>{isOn ? 'On' : 'Off'}</button>;
};
    </pre>
    This uses useState to toggle a button's state.

The <strong>useRef</strong> Hook creates a mutable reference that persists across renders without triggering re-renders. It's commonly used to access DOM elements or store values that don't affect the UI.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { useRef } from 'react';
const FocusInput = () => {
  const inputRef = useRef(null);
  const focus = () => inputRef.current.focus();
  return (
    <div>
      <input ref={inputRef} />
      <button onClick={focus}>Focus Input</button>
    </div>
  );
};
    </pre>
    This focuses an input field using useRef.

The Context API allows global state management in React by sharing data across components without prop drilling. It consists of a <strong>Provider</strong> to supply data and a <strong>Consumer</strong> (or <strong>useContext</strong> Hook) to access it.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { createContext, useContext } from 'react';
const ThemeContext = createContext();
const App = () => (
  <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">
    <Child />
  </ThemeContext.Provider>
);
const Child = () => {
  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);
  return <p>Theme: {theme}</p>;
};
    </pre>
    This shares a theme value across components.

Memoization optimizes performance by caching the results of expensive computations or preventing unnecessary re-renders. In React, it's achieved using <strong>React.memo</strong>, <strong>useMemo</strong>, and <strong>useCallback</strong>.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { useMemo } from 'react';
const ExpensiveComponent = ({ numbers }) => {
  const sum = useMemo(() => numbers.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0), [numbers]);
  return <p>Sum: {sum}</p>;
};
    </pre>
    This caches the sum of numbers until the input changes.

A <strong>controlled component</strong> has its value managed by React state, making it predictable and easier to validate. An <strong>uncontrolled component</strong> relies on the DOM for its value, using refs to access it.
    <br><br><strong>Example (Controlled):</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { useState } from 'react';
const ControlledInput = () => {
  const [value, setValue] = useState('');
  return <input value={value} onChange={e => setValue(e.target.value)} />;
};
    </pre>
    <strong>Example (Uncontrolled):</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { useRef } from 'react';
const UncontrolledInput = () => {
  const inputRef = useRef();
  return <input ref={inputRef} />;
};
    </pre>

Lazy loading defers loading of components until they're needed, improving initial load time and performance. React's <strong>React.lazy</strong> and <strong>Suspense</strong> enable this.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';
const LazyComponent = lazy(() => import('./LazyComponent'));
const App = () => (
  <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
    <LazyComponent />
  </Suspense>
);
    </pre>
    This loads LazyComponent only when rendered.

Dark mode toggles between light and dark themes, typically using CSS classes or variables and React state or Context API to manage the theme state.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { useState } from 'react';
const App = () => {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');
  return (
    <div className={theme}>
      <button onClick={() => setTheme(theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light')}>
        Toggle Theme
      </button>
    </div>
  );
};
    </pre>
    CSS: .light { background: white; } .dark { background: black; color: white; }

Redux is a predictable state management library for JavaScript apps, often used with React for managing global state in large applications. It centralizes state in a single store, using actions and reducers to update it.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import { createStore } from 'redux';
const reducer = (state = { count: 0 }, action) => {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'INCREMENT': return { count: state.count + 1 };
    default: return state;
  }
};
const store = createStore(reducer);
store.dispatch({ type: 'INCREMENT' });
    </pre>
    This increments a counter in the Redux store.

Server-Side Rendering (SSR) renders React components on the server, sending fully formed HTML to the client. This improves SEO and initial load performance. Frameworks like Next.js simplify SSR.
    <br><br><strong>Example (Next.js):</strong><br>
    <pre>
export async function getServerSideProps() {
  const data = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data').then(res => res.json());
  return { props: { data } };
}
const Page = ({ data }) => <div>{data.map(item => <p>{item.name}</p>)}</div>;
export default Page;
    </pre>
    This fetches data on the server and renders it.

Suspense allows components to "wait" for asynchronous operations (e.g., data fetching or lazy loading) while showing a fallback UI. It's commonly used with <strong>React.lazy</strong>.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { Suspense, lazy } from 'react';
const LazyComponent = lazy(() => import('./LazyComponent'));
const App = () => (
  <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
    <LazyComponent />
  </Suspense>
);
    </pre>
    This shows a loading message while LazyComponent loads.

The Context API manages global state without prop drilling, using a Provider to supply data and Consumers (or useContext) to access it. It's ideal for themes, user data, or other global values.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { createContext, useContext } from 'react';
const UserContext = createContext();
const App = () => (
  <UserContext.Provider value={{ name: 'Alice' }}>
    <User />
  </UserContext.Provider>
);
const User = () => {
  const user = useContext(UserContext);
  return <p>User: {user.name}</p>;
};
    </pre>

React Router manages navigation in React apps, enabling client-side routing. It uses components like <strong>BrowserRouter</strong>, <strong>Route</strong>, and <strong>Link</strong> to handle page transitions.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import { BrowserRouter, Route, Link } from 'react-router-dom';
const App = () => (
  <BrowserRouter>
    <nav><Link to="/about">About</Link></nav>
    <Route path="/about" component={() => <h1>About Page</h1>} />
  </BrowserRouter>
);
    </pre>

Prop drilling occurs when props are passed through multiple component layers to reach a deeply nested component, making code cumbersome. Context API or Redux can avoid this.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const Grandchild = ({ data }) => <p>{data}</p>;
const Child = ({ data }) => <Grandchild data={data} />;
const Parent = () => <Child data="Hello" />;
    </pre>
    Here, 'data' is passed through Child to Grandchild unnecessarily.

Controlled components have their form data managed by React state, ensuring predictable behavior. Uncontrolled components store data in the DOM, accessed via refs.
    <br><br><strong>Example (Controlled):</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { useState } from 'react';
const Form = () => {
  const [value, setValue] = useState('');
  return <input value={value} onChange={e => setValue(e.target.value)} />;
};
    </pre>
    <strong>Example (Uncontrolled):</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React, { useRef } from 'react';
const Form = () => {
  const inputRef = useRef();
  return <input ref={inputRef} />;
};
    </pre>

React Portals allow rendering components outside their parent DOM hierarchy, useful for modals or popups. They use <strong>ReactDOM.createPortal</strong>.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';
const Modal = ({ children }) => ReactDOM.createPortal(
  <div className="modal">{children}</div>,
  document.getElementById('modal-root')
);
    </pre>
    This renders a modal outside the main DOM tree.

React.Fragment groups multiple elements without adding extra DOM nodes, keeping the DOM clean. It's shorthand is &lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt;.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import React from 'react';
const List = () => (
  <>
    <li>Item 1</li>
    <li>Item 2</li>
  </>
);
    </pre>
    This renders two list items without a wrapper div.

Concurrent Mode is a set of React features that improve app responsiveness by allowing React to interrupt and prioritize rendering tasks. It's useful for handling complex UIs with smooth updates.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    Concurrent Mode is experimental and not fully implemented in the provided code, but it's enabled in React 18 with <strong>createRoot</strong>.
    <pre>
import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client';
const root = createRoot(document.getElementById('root'));
root.render(<App />);
    </pre>

Reconciliation is the algorithm React uses to diff a new element tree against the previous one and compute the minimal set of DOM mutations needed to update the UI. Because a full tree diff is O(n³) in the general case, React uses heuristics to make it O(n): it only compares elements of the <strong>same type</strong> at the <strong>same position</strong>, and if the type differs it tears down the old subtree and builds a new one instead of trying to reconcile it. For lists, React needs a stable identity per item to know which items were added, removed, or reordered — that's what the <strong>key</strong> prop provides. Without keys (or with array-index keys on a list that can reorder), React matches elements by position, so inserting or removing an item in the middle can cause React to misattribute state and DOM nodes to the wrong list item, leading to bugs like an input losing focus or a checkbox showing the wrong checked state after a reorder.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// Bad: index as key on a reorderable list
{items.map((item, index) => <Row key={index} {...item} />)}

// Good: stable, unique identity
{items.map((item) => <Row key={item.id} {...item} />)}
    </pre>
    With a stable id, adding a new item at the top only inserts one new DOM node; React correctly reuses the existing DOM nodes (and their internal state) for every other row instead of shifting them all down by one.

All three exist to skip work by reusing a previous result, but they operate at different levels. <strong>useMemo</strong> memoizes a computed value between renders, recomputing it only when its dependencies change — useful for expensive calculations like filtering or sorting large arrays. <strong>useCallback</strong> memoizes a function reference so it doesn't get recreated on every render — useful mainly when that function is passed as a prop to a memoized child, or used as a dependency of another hook. <strong>React.memo</strong> wraps a component so React skips re-rendering it when its props are shallow-equal to the previous render. They are cheap but not free: every memoized value or function still costs a dependency-array comparison and extra memory on every render. Overusing them on cheap components or trivial computations adds complexity and can be slower than just letting React re-render, and a poorly chosen dependency array is a common source of stale-closure bugs. Reach for them only after profiling shows a real re-render or computation cost, not by default.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// Skip this -- 'a + b' is cheap, useMemo adds overhead for nothing
const sum = useMemo(() => a + b, [a, b]);

// Worth it -- filtering 50k rows on every keystroke is not cheap
const visible = useMemo(
  () => rows.filter(r => r.name.includes(query)),
  [rows, query]
);

// useCallback only pays off when Child is wrapped in React.memo
const Child = React.memo(({ onSave }) => <button onClick={onSave}>Save</button>);
const handleSave = useCallback(() => save(id), [id]);
    </pre>
    Rule of thumb: measure first with the Profiler, then memoize the specific value or component that's actually expensive, not everything around it.

A custom hook is just a JavaScript function whose name starts with 'use' and that calls other hooks internally — it lets you extract stateful logic out of a component so it can be reused and tested independently, without changing the component tree the way a higher-order component or render prop would. Because it's a plain function, custom hooks can call other custom hooks to compose more complex behavior from small, focused pieces (e.g. a useFetch hook built from useState and useEffect, then a usePaginatedFetch built on top of useFetch). Each component that calls a custom hook gets its own independent, isolated state — the hook doesn't share state between callers unless you explicitly lift it (e.g. into Context).
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function useFetch(url) {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    let cancelled = false;
    setLoading(true);
    fetch(url)
      .then(res => res.json())
      .then(json => { if (!cancelled) setData(json); })
      .catch(err => { if (!cancelled) setError(err); })
      .finally(() => { if (!cancelled) setLoading(false); });
    return () => { cancelled = true; };
  }, [url]);

  return { data, error, loading };
}

// Composing on top of it
function useUser(id) {
  const { data, error, loading } = useFetch('/api/users/' + id);
  return { user: data, error, loading };
}
    </pre>
    The cancelled flag in the cleanup function prevents a classic bug: setting state after the component has unmounted, or after the url prop has changed to a newer request.

An Error Boundary is a component that catches JavaScript errors thrown anywhere in its child component tree during rendering, in lifecycle methods, and in constructors, logs them, and renders a fallback UI instead of crashing the whole app. Error Boundaries must currently be class components, because they rely on the <strong>static getDerivedStateFromError</strong> and <strong>componentDidCatch</strong> lifecycle methods — there is no Hook equivalent (libraries like react-error-boundary wrap this class internally so you can use it declaratively). They do not catch errors in event handlers, async code (setTimeout, promises), server-side rendering, or errors thrown in the boundary itself — those still need a plain try/catch.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  state = { hasError: false };

  static getDerivedStateFromError() {
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  componentDidCatch(error, info) {
    logErrorToService(error, info.componentStack);
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return <h2>Something went wrong.</h2>;
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}

// Usage: wrap just the risky part of the tree, not the whole app
<ErrorBoundary>
  <WidgetThatMightThrow />
</ErrorBoundary>
    </pre>
    Placing boundaries around individual widgets, rather than one boundary around the whole app, means one broken feature degrades gracefully instead of taking down the entire page.

Every component that calls useContext re-renders whenever the Provider's <strong>value</strong> changes, with no built-in way to subscribe to just a slice of that value — so a Context holding several unrelated pieces of state (e.g. user, theme, cart) re-renders every consumer whenever any of them changes, even if a component only reads theme. It's also easy to accidentally create a new object literal on every render of the Provider, which defeats memoization on every consumer below it. Context is a good fit for low-frequency, broadly-read values like theme, locale, or the current authenticated user. For state that changes often, is read by many disconnected parts of the tree, or needs selective subscriptions, a dedicated state library is usually a better fit: <strong>Zustand</strong> and <strong>Jotai</strong> use external stores with selector-based subscriptions so components only re-render for the slice they read, and <strong>Redux</strong> (with reselect/useSelector) adds a predictable, testable action and reducer flow with strong devtools for large apps with complex, cross-cutting state.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// Context: every consumer re-renders on ANY change to value
const AppContext = createContext();
function AppProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');
  // New object every render -> all consumers re-render every time
  const value = { user, setUser, theme, setTheme };
  return <AppContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</AppContext.Provider>;
}

// Zustand: components subscribe to just the slice they need
import { create } from 'zustand';
const useStore = create((set) => ({
  theme: 'light',
  toggleTheme: () => set(s => ({ theme: s.theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'light' })),
}));
function ThemeButton() {
  // Only re-renders when 'theme' changes, not on unrelated store updates
  const theme = useStore(s => s.theme);
  const toggleTheme = useStore(s => s.toggleTheme);
  return <button onClick={toggleTheme}>{theme}</button>;
}
    </pre>
    A common mitigation for Context itself is splitting one big context into several narrow ones, and memoizing the value object with useMemo — but past a certain scale, a selector-based store is simpler than juggling many contexts.

Code-splitting breaks your JS bundle into smaller chunks that load on demand instead of all upfront, shrinking the initial bundle and speeding up first paint. The most common production pattern is splitting per route: each route's component is wrapped in React.lazy(() => import(...)), so its chunk only downloads when the user navigates there, and a single Suspense boundary around the router shows a fallback while the chunk loads. Bundlers support magic comments to name the resulting chunk files for easier debugging and cache analysis. Two things to watch for in production: React.lazy only supports default exports, so named exports need a small re-export wrapper; and if the lazy chunk fails to load (e.g. a stale deployed page referencing a chunk hash that no longer exists after a new deploy), the import() promise rejects and needs to be caught by an Error Boundary with a reload-the-page fallback.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';
import { Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';

const Dashboard = lazy(() => import(/* webpackChunkName: "dashboard" */ './Dashboard'));
const Settings = lazy(() => import(/* webpackChunkName: "settings" */ './Settings'));

function App() {
  return (
    <ErrorBoundary fallback={<ChunkLoadError />}>
      <Suspense fallback={<PageSpinner />}>
        <Routes>
          <Route path="/dashboard" element={<Dashboard />} />
          <Route path="/settings" element={<Settings />} />
        </Routes>
      </Suspense>
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );
}
    </pre>
    For an even smoother UX, pair route-based splitting with prefetching on hover or intent (calling the same import() on onMouseEnter of a nav link) so the chunk is often already cached by the time the user clicks.

In SSR, the server renders React components to an HTML string that's sent to the browser for a fast first paint. <strong>Hydration</strong> is the process where React attaches on the client: it walks the existing server-rendered DOM and the client render output together, reusing the DOM nodes and wiring up event listeners, instead of throwing the HTML away and rebuilding it. A <strong>hydration mismatch</strong> happens when the markup React renders on the client doesn't match what the server sent — React then either patches the difference (with a console warning) or, in more serious cases, discards and re-renders the mismatched subtree on the client, causing a visible flash and losing the performance benefit of SSR. Common causes: using browser-only globals during render (window, localStorage, Date.now(), Math.random()) that produce different output on server vs client; conditionally rendering based on typeof window; locale or timezone-dependent formatting that differs between server and client; and invalid HTML nesting that the browser silently fixes.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// Mismatch: Date.now() differs between server render and client render
function Timestamp() {
  return <span>{new Date().toLocaleTimeString()}</span>;
}

// Fix: render a stable placeholder on first client pass, then swap in
// the real value after mount, once client and server are guaranteed
// to agree.
function Timestamp() {
  const [time, setTime] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => setTime(new Date().toLocaleTimeString()), []);
  return <span>{time ?? '--:--:--'}</span>;
}
    </pre>
    The second version intentionally renders the same placeholder on the server and on the client's first render, then updates it in a useEffect (which only runs on the client, after hydration) — this keeps the two passes in sync.

Traditional SSR renders the whole component tree to HTML on the server for the first request, but the component code itself still ships to the browser as JavaScript so it can hydrate and re-render on the client — the server just produces a head start. <strong>React Server Components</strong> (RSC), used by frameworks like Next.js App Router, introduce a new kind of component that runs only on the server: its code and dependencies never get sent to the client at all, it can read directly from a database or filesystem without an API layer, and it streams a special serialized description of its output (not HTML, and not JS) to the client, where React reconciles it with any interactive <strong>Client Components</strong> in the tree. The practical differences: RSC reduces client bundle size because server-only code and its dependencies never ship to the browser; RSC has no state, no effects, and no browser APIs, so anything interactive must be an explicit 'use client' component; and SSR alone still hydrates the entire tree on the client, while RSC lets you mix server-only and client-interactive components in the same tree and only hydrate the client parts.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// Server Component (default in the Next.js app router) -- runs only on
// the server, can await a database call directly, ships zero JS to the
// client for this component.
async function ProductList() {
  const products = await db.product.findMany();
  return (
    <ul>
      {products.map(p => <ProductRow key={p.id} product={p} />)}
      <AddToCartButton />
    </ul>
  );
}

// Client Component -- explicitly opts into hydration/interactivity
'use client';
function AddToCartButton() {
  const [added, setAdded] = useState(false);
  return <button onClick={() => setAdded(true)}>{added ? 'Added' : 'Add to cart'}</button>;
}
    </pre>
    Only AddToCartButton's code and the React runtime ship to the browser; ProductList's database query logic and any server-only dependencies never do.

Concurrent Rendering lets React prepare more than one version of the UI at the same time and interrupt a render that's in progress if something more urgent, like a keystroke or a click, comes in — rendering becomes interruptible and prioritized instead of one uninterruptible synchronous pass. This is opt-in per update: you mark a state update as low priority so React knows it's allowed to interrupt it. <strong>startTransition</strong> wraps a state update to mark it as a non-urgent transition — React keeps the old UI visible and responsive while it renders the new one in the background, and exposes an isPending flag you can use to show a loading indicator. <strong>useDeferredValue</strong> is similar but works on a value instead of a state setter — it returns a lagging copy of the value that updates once the urgent work is done, useful when you don't control the state update itself.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import { useState, useTransition, useDeferredValue, useMemo } from 'react';

function Search({ query }) {
  // Keeps typing responsive even while filtering 50k rows
  const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);
  const results = useMemo(() => filterHugeList(deferredQuery), [deferredQuery]);
  return <ResultsList results={results} />;
}

function Filters() {
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();
  const [tab, setTab] = useState('all');

  function selectTab(next) {
    startTransition(() => setTab(next)); // low priority, interruptible
  }

  return (
    <>
      {isPending && <Spinner />}
      <Tabs value={tab} onChange={selectTab} />
    </>
  );
}
    </pre>
    Neither hook makes the underlying work faster — they change scheduling, so the browser stays responsive to input while the expensive render happens in the background.

React Testing Library (RTL) is built around one guiding principle: the more your tests resemble the way your software is used, the more confidence they can give you. Instead of testing implementation details, like a component's internal state or which lifecycle methods fired, RTL pushes you to query the rendered DOM the way a real user would, by visible text, label, role, or placeholder, and interact with it the way a user would (clicking, typing), then assert on what the user would actually see. This makes tests resilient to refactors: renaming an internal state variable, switching from a class component to a function component, or changing how a value is computed shouldn't break a test, because none of that is user-observable. It also naturally nudges you toward accessible markup, since getByRole and getByLabelText only work well when your components use semantic HTML and proper ARIA attributes.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';
import LoginForm from './LoginForm';

test('shows an error when submitting with an empty password', async () => {
  const user = userEvent.setup();
  render(<LoginForm />);

  await user.type(screen.getByLabelText(/email/i), 'a@b.com');
  await user.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /log in/i }));

  expect(await screen.findByText(/password is required/i)).toBeInTheDocument();
});
    </pre>
    Notice the test never touches component internals, no shallow rendering, no checking this.state.error — it only queries what a user could see and does what a user could do.

The React DevTools browser extension has a <strong>Profiler</strong> tab that records a session of renders (start recording, interact with the app, stop) and shows a flame graph or ranked chart per commit: which components rendered, how long each took, and, critically, why each one rendered (props changed, state changed, hooks changed, parent re-rendered, or context changed). Enabling 'Highlight updates when components render' in the Components tab flashes a colored outline around any component that re-renders, which is a fast way to visually spot a component re-rendering far more often than it should. Once you've found a hot spot, the usual fixes are: memoizing the component with React.memo if its props are usually the same, memoizing an expensive prop with useMemo or useCallback so React.memo's shallow comparison actually succeeds, moving state closer to where it's used, or splitting a context that changes often away from one that doesn't.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// Before profiling you might guess wrongly; the Profiler shows the
// truth. A common finding: a Provider's value object is recreated
// every render, so every consumer re-renders even though the data
// didn't actually change.
function CartProvider({ children }) {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
  // Bug: new object identity every render defeats memoized consumers
  // const value = { items, setItems };

  // Fix: stable identity unless items/setItems actually change
  const value = useMemo(() => ({ items, setItems }), [items]);
  return <CartContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</CartContext.Provider>;
}
    </pre>
    Always profile a production-like build (npm run build plus a static server), not the dev server — the dev build includes extra checks that make renders look slower than they really are.

A fully controlled form, where every input's value is driven by React state, gives you a single source of truth that's easy to validate, format, and sync — but on a form with dozens or hundreds of fields, every keystroke in any field triggers a re-render of whatever component owns that state, and if the state lives high up, it can re-render the whole form on every character typed. Uncontrolled inputs, using refs and reading the value only on submit or blur, avoid that per-keystroke re-render entirely, at the cost of losing live validation and formatting and making the current value harder to read programmatically. In practice, large production forms rarely pick one extreme: libraries like <strong>react-hook-form</strong> use uncontrolled inputs under the hood, subscribing to the DOM via refs, and only re-render the specific field or fields that need to update, giving controlled-like ergonomics (validation, error messages, watch()) without a re-render per keystroke across the whole form.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// Naively controlled: every keystroke in ANY field re-renders the
// whole form component and re-runs its validation function.
function BigFormNaive() {
  const [values, setValues] = useState({ /* 60 fields */ });
  const onChange = (name) => (e) =>
    setValues(v => ({ ...v, [name]: e.target.value }));
  // ...60 <input value={values.x} onChange={onChange('x')} /> fields
}

// react-hook-form: inputs are uncontrolled (registered via ref);
// only the field(s) that actually need re-rendering do.
import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form';
function BigFormRHF() {
  const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors } } = useForm();
  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(save)}>
      <input {...register('email', { required: true })} />
      {errors.email && <span>Email is required</span>}
    </form>
  );
}
    </pre>
    Rule of thumb: reach for controlled inputs for small, simple forms where re-renders are cheap; reach for an uncontrolled or hybrid approach once a form grows large enough that per-keystroke re-renders become measurably janky.

Compound components are a set of components that work together to form one cohesive UI unit, sharing implicit state via Context instead of the parent explicitly wiring every prop down to every child (think select/option, or antd's Collapse/Collapse.Panel). The parent owns the shared state and exposes it through Context; each child subscribes to just what it needs. This gives consumers a very flexible, declarative API: they control composition and order, can insert their own markup between the pieces, and don't have to pass a big config object of props, while the components still coordinate correctly under the hood.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
const TabsContext = createContext();

function Tabs({ defaultValue, children }) {
  const [active, setActive] = useState(defaultValue);
  return (
    <TabsContext.Provider value={{ active, setActive }}>
      <div className="tabs">{children}</div>
    </TabsContext.Provider>
  );
}

Tabs.List = function TabList({ children }) {
  return <div className="tab-list">{children}</div>;
};

Tabs.Tab = function Tab({ value, children }) {
  const { active, setActive } = useContext(TabsContext);
  return (
    <button className={active === value ? 'active' : ''} onClick={() => setActive(value)}>
      {children}
    </button>
  );
};

// Usage -- the consumer freely composes the pieces:
<Tabs defaultValue="profile">
  <Tabs.List>
    <Tabs.Tab value="profile">Profile</Tabs.Tab>
    <Tabs.Tab value="billing">Billing</Tabs.Tab>
  </Tabs.List>
</Tabs>
    </pre>
    Reach for this pattern when a component family has a handful of tightly related pieces that all need to share state, and you want the consumer to control layout and composition rather than passing it via a rigid props API.

Before Hooks existed (pre-16.8), the only way to reuse stateful logic across components was to reuse a component itself. Two patterns emerged for that: a <strong>Higher-Order Component</strong> is a function that takes a component and returns a new component that wraps it with extra props or behavior (e.g. withRouter, connect from Redux). A <strong>Render Prop</strong> is a component that takes a function as a prop, often called render or passed as children, and calls it with some state, letting the consumer decide what to render with that state. Both work, but both add an extra component to the tree, which shows up in DevTools as 'wrapper hell' when you compose several of them, they make prop names collide or shadow each other in non-obvious ways, and they make the data flow harder to trace statically. <strong>Hooks</strong> solve the same reuse problem without adding any component to the tree at all — a custom hook is just a function call inside your component, so composing five hooks adds zero wrapper components and the data each one returns is named and typed explicitly at the call site.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
// HOC: adds a wrapper component, props arrive "by magic"
function withMousePosition(Component) {
  return function Wrapped(props) {
    const [pos, setPos] = useState({ x: 0, y: 0 });
    useEffect(() => {
      const handler = (e) => setPos({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });
      window.addEventListener('mousemove', handler);
      return () => window.removeEventListener('mousemove', handler);
    }, []);
    return <Component {...props} mousePosition={pos} />;
  };
}
const TrackedComp = withMousePosition(MyComponent);

// Hook: same logic, no extra component, explicit at the call site
function useMousePosition() {
  const [pos, setPos] = useState({ x: 0, y: 0 });
  useEffect(() => {
    const handler = (e) => setPos({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });
    window.addEventListener('mousemove', handler);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('mousemove', handler);
  }, []);
  return pos;
}
function MyComponent() {
  const { x, y } = useMousePosition();
  return <p>{x}, {y}</p>;
}
    </pre>
    HOCs and render props aren't wrong, and some libraries still expose them for backward compatibility, but for new code a custom hook is almost always the simpler, flatter way to share stateful logic.

useSyncExternalStore is the official Hook, added in React 18, for subscribing a component to a value that lives outside React's own state — a store from a library like Redux or Zustand, a browser API like window.innerWidth, or any other external mutable source. Before it existed, libraries subscribed to external stores with a useEffect plus useState combo, which has a subtle correctness problem under React 18's concurrent rendering: the external store can change in between when React starts rendering and when it commits, causing the UI to briefly show a torn state where different parts of the same render reflect different versions of the store. useSyncExternalStore forces a synchronous, consistent read of the store during render and forces React to re-render immediately if the store changed since the render started, guaranteeing every component in a single commit sees the same snapshot. Most app code never calls it directly, since it's the low-level primitive that state-management libraries use internally, but it's worth knowing when integrating a non-React data source directly.
    <br><br><strong>Example:</strong><br>
    <pre>
import { useSyncExternalStore } from 'react';

function subscribe(callback) {
  window.addEventListener('resize', callback);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', callback);
}

function useWindowWidth() {
  return useSyncExternalStore(
    subscribe,
    () => window.innerWidth,       // client snapshot
    () => 0                        // server snapshot (SSR-safe default)
  );
}

function Layout() {
  const width = useWindowWidth();
  return <div>{width < 768 ? <MobileNav /> : <DesktopNav />}</div>;
}
    </pre>
    The third argument, the server snapshot, is what makes this SSR-safe — it avoids reading window during server rendering, sidestepping the hydration-mismatch problem entirely.
